---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/tribe_conversion.json';

// Mechanic transcribed from Tile.doTribeTurn (Tile.cs ~11372) and
// Game.findNearestSettlementTribe (Game.cs:15111). Numbers from tribeLevel.xml
// via build_tribe_conversion.py.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { levels, search, adopters, barbarian, counts } = data as any;
const converting = levels.filter((l: any) => l.canConvert);
const milestones = converting[0]?.odds.map((o: any) => o.turnsAfter) ?? [];
---

<Base
  title="Barbarian Site Conversion"
  active="tribe-conversion"
  pageMark="🏕️"
  description="How barbarian sites become tribal sites in Old World: from a set turn onward each barbarian settlement rolls a few percent per turn to be adopted by the nearest real tribe within 12 tiles — but only if no player unit and no damaged barbarian is nearby. Start turns and per-turn odds by tribe level, transcribed from Tile.doTribeTurn."
  pageStats={[
    { label: 'levels that convert', value: `${counts.convertingLevels} of ${levels.length}` },
    { label: 'search range', value: `${search.range} tiles` },
    { label: 'adopting tribes', value: counts.adopters },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Barbarian camps are not always barbarian forever. Once a game is past a certain turn, every
    barbarian settlement rolls each turn to be <b>adopted by the nearest real tribe</b> — the site
    changes hands, and the barbarians standing on it become that tribe's soldiers. It is rare
    because the roll is a few percent and the site has to be <em>quiet</em> at that moment.
    Transcribed from <code>Tile.doTribeTurn</code> and
    <code>Game.findNearestSettlementTribe</code>; numbers from <code>tribeLevel.xml</code>.
  </p>

  <section class="tc">
    <h2 class="tc__h">The roll, step by step</h2>
    <ol class="tc__steps">
      <li><b>Only barbarian sites are eligible.</b> The site's tribe must be one that
        <em>cannot</em> do diplomacy — {barbarian.map((b: any) => b.name).join(', ')}. A site
        already held by a real tribe never re-rolls.</li>
      <li><b>The clock has to have started.</b> Nothing happens until the turn passes the tribe
        level's start turn (below). Before that, the check is skipped entirely.</li>
      <li><b>Roll the percent</b>, once per site per turn.</li>
      <li><b>Find an adopter:</b> the nearest tribe — by actual pathfinding distance, land or
        water — with a settlement within <b>{search.range} tiles</b>. That tribe must be alive
        and diplomacy-capable. No tribe in range, no conversion.</li>
      <li><b>The site must be quiet.</b> Scanning every tile within
        <b>{converting[0]?.unitRange ?? 3}</b> of the site: if <em>any</em> player unit is
        present, or any barbarian of that site is <em>damaged</em>, the conversion is
        cancelled for that turn.</li>
      <li><b>The flip.</b> The site becomes the new tribe's, trade networks rebuild, and each
        barbarian in range is re-created under the new tribe — or, if it has a city to strike
        from where it stands, converted straight into a <b>raider</b> against that city.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="tc__h">When it can happen, and how likely</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Both the start turn and the per-turn chance come from the game's <b>Tribe Level</b>
      setting. The cumulative columns are the odds that one undisturbed site has flipped after
      that many eligible turns — <code>1 − (1 − p)ⁿ</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="grid"><div class="grid__scroll">
      <table class="ntbl tc__tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="rowlabel rowlabel--corner">Tribe level</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Starts after turn</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Chance / turn</th>
            {milestones.map((n: number) => (
              <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">after {n} turns</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {levels.map((l: any) => (
            <tr class={l.canConvert ? '' : 'tc__off'}>
              <td class="rowlabel tc__name">{l.name}</td>
              {l.canConvert ? (
                <>
                  <td class="cell cell--num">{l.startTurn}</td>
                  <td class="cell cell--num tc__pct">{l.percent}%</td>
                  {l.odds.map((o: any) => (
                    <td class="cell cell--num">{o.chance}%<span class="tc__sub">by turn {o.byTurn}</span></td>
                  ))}
                </>
              ) : (
                <td class="cell cell--num tc__never" colspan={2 + milestones.length}>never converts</td>
              )}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div></div>
    <p class="tc__note">
      Higher tribe levels convert <b>earlier and faster</b> — Raging starts 30 turns before
      Weak does and rolls twice as often. On <b>Passive</b> and with tribes off, sites never
      convert at all, which is why the mechanic is easy to play hundreds of hours without
      noticing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tc__h">Who can adopt a site</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Only the {counts.adopters} tribes the game lets you negotiate with — the ones with a
      <code>bDiplomacy</code> flag. Each must already have a settlement of its own within range
      to reach out from.
    </p>
    <div class="tc__tribes">
      {adopters.map((t: any) => (
        <span class="tc__tribe">{t.name}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="tc__note">
      The practical consequence: a barbarian camp near an established tribe is on borrowed time,
      and when it flips you gain a neighbour you can <em>talk to</em> — trade, truce, alliance —
      where before there was only a permanent enemy. Keeping a unit parked next to the camp
      blocks the conversion indefinitely, and so does leaving one of its defenders wounded.
    </p>

    <p class="tc__see">
      See also <a href={`${base}tribes`}>Tribes</a> and
      <a href={`${base}camp-spawning`}>Camp Unit Spawning</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { max-width: 88ch; color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; }
  .lede b { color: var(--text); }
  .lede code, .tc code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85em; background: var(--bg-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.03rem 0.3rem; color: var(--text-soft); }

  .tc__h { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--accent); margin: 1.6rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .tc__h:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .tc__intro, .tc__note { max-width: 88ch; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 0.8rem; }
  .tc__note { color: var(--text-faint); margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .tc__intro b, .tc__note b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__steps { max-width: 88ch; margin: 0 0 0.6rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.55; }
  .tc__steps b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__tbl { max-width: 62rem; }
  .tc__name { text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text); }
  .nhdr--num, .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { text-align: right; }
  .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: 0.84rem; vertical-align: top; }
  .tc__pct { color: var(--gold); }
  .tc__sub { display: block; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.58rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .tc__never { color: var(--text-faint); font-style: italic; text-align: left !important; }
  .tc__off .tc__name { color: var(--text-faint); }

  .tc__tribes { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .tc__tribe { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; }

  .tc__see { margin-top: 1.4rem; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .tc__see a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
